## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end design for real-time monocular 6-DoF pose with few-shot adaptation on edge hardware, including data strategy and robust evaluation.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time monocular 6-DoF pose estimation
- Lightweight backbone plus specialized heads for detection, segmentation, and pose
- On-device few-shot adaptation via adapters or compact fine-tuning
- Synthetic+real data and domain randomization
- Occlusion robustness and reprojection-based losses

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: simple pose reprojection loss sketch
function poseLoss(pred, gt, intr) {
  // pred: 6-DoF [rx,ry,rz,tx,ty,tz], gt: ground truth
  // project 3D corners to 2D and compare
  const pts3D = corners3D();
  const pts2D = project(pts3D, pred, intr);
  return meanSquaredError(pts2D, gt2D);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure adaptation effectiveness on unseen feature types with minimal labeling?
- What strategies mitigate drift when features are tiny or heavily occluded?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design real-time non-rigid 3D reconstruction from monocular input, integrating differentiable rendering and on-device adaptation—critical for AR try-on. It also probes data strategy, evaluation, and edge-latency trade-offs in a commercial retail context.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular non-rigid 3D reconstruction with deformable garments
- Differentiable rendering and photometric consistency
- Temporal fusion for stability
- 5-shot on-device adaptation for unseen fabrics
- Edge latency and data strategy (synthetic+real)

## Code Example
```javascript
function forward(frame, params){
  const latent = encoder(frame, params.enc);
  const mesh = deformableMesh(latent, params.shape);
  const render = renderer(mesh, params.tex);
  const loss = photometric(render, frame) + temporal(mesh);
  return {mesh, loss};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle occlusion or loose fabrics?
- How evaluate mesh quality vs. latency?
- How would you construct synthetic data for textiles?
